    "title": "Artemis II re-entry may cause sonic boom along San Diego County coast Friday",
    "text": "NASA says that people along the coast of San Diego County might hear a sonic boom Friday afternoon when the Orion capsule carrying the Artemis II crew re-enters the atmosphere, wrapping up its historic trip around the moon. The capsule is expected to re-enter at 4:54 p.m. when it is about 400,000 feet above Earth’s surface, and will parachute into the ocean 50 to 60 miles west of San Diego at 5:07 p.m. The boom could be loud enough to shake windows in some areas. Skies will be partly cloudy, making it unlikely that people will catch a glimpse of the capsule and its 11 parachutes. The four astronauts will be guided out of the capsule by a recovery team from the USS John P. Murtha, which will be a short distance away, preparing to transport the crew by helicopter to Naval Air Station North Island. The crew will then be flown to Houston by jet. A sonic boom could be a learning moment for UC San Diego’s Scripps Institution of Oceanography, which will place acoustic monitoring equipment off the coast to help Johns Hopkins University study the nature of such phenomena. “Scientists will use the data to study how the acoustic shock in air propagates into the ocean and seabed, where it could potentially propagate outwards for hundreds of kilometers,” Scripps said in a statement on Thursday. “This would enable distant, sparsely placed sensors to detect and even track space vehicles and debris as they re-enter the atmosphere.”",
